Diagnostic: what a glyph run's fill was actually given, and a switch to take
the other path

Two measurement-only things in `d2d_device_context_draw_glyph_run*`.

**One TRACE**, on the d2d channel, at the point Wine's own glyph-run renderer
is about to fill: the destination rectangle, the alpha-texture bounds, the
transform, the clip it has to survive, the brush, the mask size, and how many
bytes of that mask the rasteriser actually set.

**One switch**, `WINE_ALTARS_GLYPH_OUTLINE=1`, which sends every run down the
outline path (filled glyph geometry) instead of the bitmap path (an A8 coverage
mask through a bitmap brush).  Two entirely different rasterisation and fill
routes, one environment variable apart, is the cheapest way to ask whether a
missing glyph is missing *because of* the path it took.  Answer, for Word's
Backstage rail: no -- the outline path loses exactly the same labels, and the
title bar comes out worse with it (7 distinct colours against the usual 81).

It exists to answer a question that had four plausible answers and no
measurement.  Word's Backstage navigation rail comes up with no icons and no
labels, and the "新建" heading, the 最近/收藏夹/与我共享 tabs and the search box
with them, while the template captions beside them render.  `+d2d` already
showed that every missing string reaches `DrawGlyphRun` -- but "reaches
DrawGlyphRun" is a long way from "was drawn", and each of these could have
been true:

  - DirectWrite rasterised nothing (an empty alpha texture fills nothing and
    leaves no trace of itself);
  - the fill landed outside its clip (the function sets the transform to
    identity for the fill while the clip was pushed under a transform);
  - the brush was missing or transparent;
  - everything Wine does is right and the loss is past it.

Measured, one Word start-up, 124 glyph-run fills:

    Word   coverage 161/320    clip (16,0)-(49,16)    rect (16,3)-(48,13)
    开始   coverage 202/264    clip (129,0)-(155,16)  rect (130,3)-(154,14)
    新建   coverage 202/288    clip (155,0)-(181,16)  rect (156,2)-(180,14)
    打开   coverage 126/288    clip (181,0)-(207,16)  rect (182,3)-(206,15)
    帐户   coverage 137/253
    反馈   coverage 179/288
    选项   coverage 176/264
    空白文档 coverage 384/576   (this one does appear on screen)

    all-zero masks in the whole run: 0

Every rectangle is inside its clip, every transform is the pure translation
that clip was pushed under, every brush is real, and every mask carries
coverage -- for the labels that never appear exactly as much as for the ones
that do.  **So the whole of Wine's text path is doing its job for them**, and
the fourth answer is the one left: whatever loses these labels is past
`d2d_device_context_fill_geometry`, in Office's own compositing of the atlas
slots it just filled.  That is where the next session should start, and it is
a different instrument -- Office's D3D11 draws, not Wine's D2D calls.

Guarded by TRACE_ON(d2d), so it costs a branch when the channel is off; the
coverage count walks the mask, which is why it is inside the guard rather
than computed unconditionally.
--- a/dlls/d2d1/device.c
+++ b/dlls/d2d1/device.c
@@ -1522,6 +1522,31 @@ static void d2d_device_context_draw_glyph_run_bitmap(struct d2d_device_context *
 
     m = *transform;
     *transform = identity;
+    /* altars diagnostic: the glyph run's own destination against the clip it
+     * has to survive.  Word's Backstage rail labels go through this function
+     * with no error and never appear; the two rectangles here, printed next to
+     * the run's text (which +d2d prints immediately above), are what says
+     * whether the fill was ever inside the clip. */
+    if (TRACE_ON(d2d))
+    {
+        const D2D1_RECT_F *clip = context->clip_stack.count
+                ? &context->clip_stack.stack[context->clip_stack.count - 1] : NULL;
+
+        size_t i, set = 0;
+
+        /* How much coverage the rasteriser actually produced.  An all-zero
+         * mask fills nothing and looks identical, from every other vantage
+         * point in this function, to a fill that was dropped later. */
+        for (i = 0; i < opacity_values_size; ++i)
+            if (((const unsigned char *)opacity_values)[i]) ++set;
+
+        TRACE("altars-glyphfill run_rect %s, bounds %s, transform {%.2f %.2f %.2f %.2f %.2f %.2f}, "
+                "clip %s (depth %u), brush %p, mask %ux%u, coverage %zu/%zu.\n",
+                debug_d2d_rect_f(&run_rect), wine_dbgstr_rect(&bounds),
+                m._11, m._12, m._21, m._22, m._31, m._32,
+                clip ? debug_d2d_rect_f(clip) : "none", context->clip_stack.count,
+                brush, bitmap_size.width, bitmap_size.height, set, opacity_values_size);
+    }
     d2d_device_context_fill_geometry(context, unsafe_impl_from_ID2D1Geometry((ID2D1Geometry *)geometry),
             unsafe_impl_from_ID2D1Brush(brush), unsafe_impl_from_ID2D1Brush((ID2D1Brush *)opacity_brush));
     *transform = m;
@@ -1626,6 +1651,20 @@ static void d2d_device_context_draw_glyph_run(struct d2d_device_context *context
         }
     }
 
+    /* altars experiment: WINE_ALTARS_GLYPH_OUTLINE=1 sends every run down the
+     * outline path instead, which fills glyph geometry directly rather than
+     * going through an A8 coverage mask and a bitmap brush.  It answers, in
+     * one run, whether the labels Word never shows are lost inside the bitmap
+     * path or somewhere past it. */
+    {
+        static int force_outline = -1;
+
+        if (force_outline < 0)
+            force_outline = !!getenv("WINE_ALTARS_GLYPH_OUTLINE");
+        if (force_outline)
+            rendering_mode = DWRITE_RENDERING_MODE_OUTLINE;
+    }
+
     if (rendering_mode == DWRITE_RENDERING_MODE_OUTLINE)
         d2d_device_context_draw_glyph_run_outline(context, baseline_origin, glyph_run, brush);
     else
